00:21:31,830 --> 00:21:40,549
Codexs are typically not relative free and so browsers decide which codecs they're going to support, which audio and video codecs they they're going to support.

201
00:21:40,559 --> 00:21:48,230
And doing that that creates additional fragmentation, not in the sense that they're not interoperable,

202
00:21:48,230 --> 00:21:56,070
but in the sense that applications need to choose which codec they're going to ship the stream to the end user.

203
00:21:56,070 --> 00:21:59,510
And uh it's all the more complicated that some codecs are going to be hardware supported.

204
00:21:59,510 --> 00:22:06,149
So you will have a hardware decoder in your laptop or smartphone and so that's going to be efficient to decode some stream

205
00:22:06,159 --> 00:22:15,669
whereas some codecs are are going to be softwarebased supported and that may consume a lot of CPU and a lot of power and a lot of energy in the end.

206
00:22:15,669 --> 00:22:19,990
So you you'll want to avoid that if you can select another thing.

207
00:22:19,990 --> 00:22:26,390
And it's even more complex than codecs have different profiles, lower end profiles, higher end profiles with different capabilities,

208
00:22:26,400 --> 00:22:34,630
different features depending on whether you're going to use this or that color space, for example, this or that resolution, whatever.

209
00:22:34,630 --> 00:22:43,990
And so you want to suffice that to web applications because otherwise they can't select they can't choose the right codec and the right stream

210
00:22:43,990 --> 00:22:56,390
that they're going to send to the uh client devices and so they're not going to provide an efficient user experience first and even a sustainable one in terms of energy because they're they're going to waste energy if they don't send the right stream.

211
00:22:56,390 --> 00:23:02,950
So you want to suffice that to application that's what the media capabilities API provides.

